\begin{abstract}
Migrating deep learning training code across frameworks is harder than ordinary source-code translation because a translated program must preserve execution behavior, forward numerics, gradients, and parameter updates. Existing code-translation and repair systems increasingly use executable feedback, but their validation signals are often too coarse for framework migration: a candidate may run successfully while silently changing training semantics. This paper formulates PyTorch-to-MindSpore training-code migration as a feedback-design problem and introduces a hierarchical validator with execution, numerical, and gradient/update signals. We instantiate the design in a prototype built on a torch4ms-backed execution path and evaluate it on controlled paired training anchors, controlled fault injection, and short-horizon training trajectories. On a 12-task sentinel set, execution-only feedback repairs 4/12 tasks, execution-plus-numerical feedback repairs 8/12, and the full hierarchy repairs 12/12 under strict re-verification. In a separate 24-base-task multi-scope ablation, binary feedback repairs no tasks, whereas layered feedback repairs 18/24 without the Fixer guide and 14/24 with it; no condition repairs the core autograd/optimizer tasks. A one-shot Translator ablation raises Translation Success@1 from 0/15 without platform guidance to 15/15 with it, while the final real-LLM Fixer evaluation repairs 46/50 instances across 25 fault categories. All 12 oracle-candidate runs in the selected 50-step consistency bundle pass the studied loss, gradient-norm, and parameter-update thresholds. The results support hierarchical feedback as a useful diagnostic layer for framework migration on controlled tasks and short-horizon training trajectories.
\end{abstract}

