\textbf{Results.}
Table~\ref{tab:feedback-ablation} shows a stepwise improvement as the repair loop receives the semantic layer needed for each fault class. Execution-only feedback repaired 4 of 12 tasks under strict verification, execution plus numerical feedback repaired 8 of 12, and the full feedback stack repaired all 12 tasks. The same pattern appears in Repair@4, which increased from 33.3\% to 66.7\% and then to 100.0\%. The fault-type breakdown in Table~\ref{tab:feedback-by-fault} explains the trend. Runtime evidence was sufficient for the four execution faults across all conditions. Numerical faults were not repaired with execution-only feedback, but all four were repaired once numerical diagnostics were exposed. Gradient/update faults remained unsolved until gradient and parameter-update feedback was included, after which all four gradient/update tasks were repaired.

These results support a bounded claim. On this sentinel ablation set, each added feedback layer enabled the fixer to recover the corresponding class of hidden training-semantics failure. The experiment should not be read as the final absolute repair-rate estimate: Section~\ref{sec:fixer-capability} evaluates the final configuration on a broader 25-category, 50-instance fault pool. Instead, this ablation isolates why the hierarchy is useful: execution feedback handles runnable failures, numerical feedback exposes forward-semantic faults, and gradient/update feedback is necessary for backward or optimizer-state faults that execution and loss signals alone do not localize.


\subsection{Platform-Guidance Ablations}
We separate guidance for the iterative Fixer from guidance for the one-shot Translator because the two components have different inputs, actions, and evaluation scopes.

\subsubsection{Multi-Scope Feedback by Fixer-Guide Ablation}
\label{sec:fixer-guide-ablation}
\textbf{Design.}
