Table~\ref{tab:signal-effectiveness} shows that the expected signal detects its corresponding fault in all 36 controlled fault-injection runs. Execution faults are isolated by the execution signal. In the archived training-fault rows, the checker combines a missing candidate gradient comparison with a parameter-update relative $\ell_2$ difference of 1.0; thus G denotes missing-gradient evidence there, not an observed gradient-norm difference. Numerical faults are detected by the loss-difference criterion and often propagate to gradient or update metrics. The signals are therefore not mutually exclusive in their effects, but they provide different first diagnostic handles.

\subsubsection{Final Fixer Evaluation on a Fixed Fault Pool}
\label{sec:fixer-capability}
\textbf{Design.}
We next evaluated whether the recorded verifier diagnoses and real-LLM fixer could be turned into repairs. This experiment used a fixed pool of 25 controlled migration-fault categories and 50 model-assigned instances. The pool covered four model families (CNN, image MLP, Transformer classifier, and tiny causal LM), three failure layers, and three repair scopes: candidate-level code, core operators, and core autograd/optimizer behavior. The result bundle marks all 50 instances as blind. Each instance allowed at most four repair attempts. Strict success required the repaired artifact to pass the original fault oracle and strict re-verification; failures were restored or rolled back.

