\subsection{Limitations}
The evidence is intentionally bounded: the repair ablations use small controlled tasks, and the signal-effectiveness experiment uses controlled fault injection. The current checker can score gradient/update evidence when a gradient comparison is missing but an update comparison is present; future versions should require complete metrics and rerun the repair ablations. The selected artifacts lack a shared verifier-version hash, a fixed-50 prompt/redaction manifest, and per-trajectory dataset/batch identifiers. The 50-step bundle uses oracle candidates, so it supports short-horizon path consistency rather than arbitrary generated-output quality. The Translator-guide result uses one LLM and prompt configuration, while the latest Fixer-guide ablation does not show a strict-success benefit; broader configurations are needed before generalizing guide effects.

\section{Conclusion}

This paper studied DL framework migration as a feedback-design problem. We proposed a hierarchical validation mechanism that separates execution, forward numerical, and gradient/update signals, then used it to guide PyTorch-to-MindSpore training-code migration repair on a \texttt{torch4ms}-backed prototype. The experiments show that layered feedback improves repair outcomes under the recorded strict outcomes and that a platform-specific guide can improve one-shot Translator output in the tested configuration. The latest multi-scope run does not show a strict-success benefit from applying the guide to the Fixer, and the selected 50-step bundle establishes short-horizon numerical consistency for the covered paths. The verifier analysis further motivates treating missing observations explicitly rather than silently scoring only the metrics that are present. The results support hierarchical feedback as a useful diagnostic layer for training-code migration. Future work should harden the verifier against missing metrics, archive versioned verifier and prompt manifests, broaden the benchmark, evaluate generated candidates over longer training horizons, improve layer-level localization for complex models, and test the design across additional framework pairs.

